# Database migrations

This pre-deployment release starts from an empty database with one initial
revision, `4f73ae3ff827`. The migration creates schema, not users or catalogue
records. Sources supply catalogue data; registration and explicit administrator
bootstrap create accounts. Bundled synthetic fixtures are opt-in development/test
inputs, not deployment data.

Provision the empty database and run the manual migration unit plus runtime
grants before starting web or scheduler. Use migration owner credentials and
the commands in [Deployment](../../Deployment.md); runtime roles cannot run DDL.
Recreate disposable databases made from an earlier baseline body: `upgrade head`
does not rerun an already recorded revision, and stamping cannot install missing
objects. Application/updater tools never drop or recreate databases.

The baseline owns literal snapshots of its catalogue function bodies and search
candidate selector. Historical revisions must not import current `app` or
`config` code to generate DDL. `src/app/catalogue_sql.py` and the runtime schema
contract describe the currently required database behavior; they do not define
what an already-numbered revision creates.

The current search-mask protocol is defined in `src/app/search_mask_contract.py`.
Its page-function source and complete count-view definition must be frozen
together when preparing a schema change. Runtime SQL is generated from the
versioned field map; the historical revision imports neither that map nor its
renderer. This pre-deployment reset updates the initial literal snapshots,
includes the nullable `source_fetch_cursor` and `source_fetch_fingerprint` columns
in `CREATE TABLE sync_status`, and updates the pinned upgrade SQL digest while
retaining revision `4f73ae3ff827`. New source rows start with NULL clean and
consumed cursors/fingerprints; there is no retained-data backfill.

`src/tests/unit/test_migration_history.py` pins the exact ordered upgrade and
downgrade SQL for this baseline, rejects application/configuration imports in
revision files, and verifies that runtime SQL changes cannot alter replay.
Do not regenerate snapshots or update those digests during builds or startup.

Before the first retained deployment, an explicitly reviewed baseline reset may
change the initial schema, its literal snapshots and the pinned SQL digests
together. The operator must recreate any database made from the older baseline;
the revision label alone cannot identify that older body. No reset is performed
by application tools. Once a retained deployment exists, preserve historical
revisions and add a new reviewed revision for every subsequent schema change.

For a future catalogue SQL change, update the current runtime SQL/contract and
add a revision containing its own literal `CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION` or view
DDL. A downgrade must restore the previous literal definition. Rehearse both
upgrade from the previous head and installation from `base`; then run live
schema validation against the new head. Update intentional migration-head pins
in wheel-smoke and backup/recovery checks, plus grants and recovery procedures
when affected. A test must not require an old revision to equal the latest
runtime SQL: newer revisions are responsible for advancing the schema.

Downgrading to `base` deletes all application data and removes `pg_trgm`.
